      "observation": "The acoustic system governs pressure p and particle velocity u in a one-dimensional medium. Pressure more precisely denotes a perturbation from a constant background pressure. K and ρ are material constants, respectively the bulk modulus of compressibility and density."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2; paragraph following equation (1.6)",
      "anchor": "where c = √(K/ρ)",
      "observation": "The sound speed c is defined from the same material constants used in the acoustic system."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2; displayed equations for w¹ and w²",
      "anchor": "w¹ = p + ρcu; w² = p − ρcu",
      "observation": "The surrounding discussion identifies c as the speed of sound and describes the two acoustic propagation directions through speeds c and −c."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; equation (1.7)",
      "anchor": "second-order wave equation; p_tt = c² p_xx",
      "observation": "The selected equation is a second-order wave equation for the pressure variable."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; paragraph immediately after equation (1.7)",
      "anchor": "differentiating the first equation with respect to t and the second with respect to x",
      "observation": "The source claims that equation (1.7) follows from system (1.5) by these differentiations and elimination of the mixed derivatives of u."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; paragraph immediately after equation (1.7)",
      "anchor": "also called a hyperbolic equation according to the standard classification of second-order linear equations",
      "observation": "The source classifies equation (1.7) as hyperbolic and distinguishes this second-order classification from its principal treatment of first-order hyperbolic systems."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "Spatial coordinate x and time coordinate t are real variables in the enclosing one-dimensional PDE framework; equation (1.7) suppresses their arguments [printed page 1, equation (1.1) and q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝ^m; printed page 3, equation (1.7)].",
      "p(x,t) and u(x,t) are scalar pressure and particle-velocity fields, respectively [printed page 2, equation (1.5) and following definitions].",
